      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pungoteague%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1665, William Darby staged \"Ye Bear and Ye Cub\" at Cowle's Tavern, the first English-language play in the colonies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pungoteague%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "First meetings of the Pungoteague Episcopal congregation began in 1636, with a church built between 1666 and 1676.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pungoteague%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1736, the original frame church was replaced by a brick structure in Flemish bond pattern.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pungoteague%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During the mid‑19th century, Pungoteague sat on the stagecoach line between Wilmington, Delaware and Eastville, Virginia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pungoteague%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Pungoteague’s post office opened in 1817, long before the town appeared on modern maps.",
